{"amatra": [{"id": "amatra", "headword": "अमत्र", "headword_slp1": "amatra", "fold": "amatra", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Amatra was a vessel into which the soma, after being pressed, was poured, and out of which the libation to the god was made.\nFoot notes Rv. ii. 14, 1; v. 51, 4; vi. 42, 2, etc.\nRv. x. 29, 7.\nCf. Zimmer, Altindisches Leben, 278; Oldenberg, ṛgveda Noten, 1, 61.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["amatra", "అమత్ర", "ಅಮತ್ರ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["amatra", "అమత్ర", "ಅಮತ್ರ"]}], "amala": [{"id": "amalA", "headword": "अमला", "headword_slp1": "amalA", "fold": "amala", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Amalā.--This plant is probably the Emblica officinalis, or Myrobalan tree; also called āmalaka, or Āmalakā.\nFoot notes Jaiminīya upaniṣad brāhmaṇa, i. 38, 6.\nThe reading in Chāndogya upaniṣad, vii. 3, 1, is vāmalake, which may give a¤° or ā°.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["amalaa", "amalā", "అమలా", "ಅಮಲಾ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["amalaa", "amalā", "అమలా", "ಅಮಲಾ"]}], "ama-jur": [{"id": "amA-jur", "headword": "अमा-जुर्", "headword_slp1": "amA-jur", "fold": "ama-jur", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Amā-jur is an epithet denoting maidens ‘who grow old at home’ without finding husbands, or, as they are elsewhere called, ‘who sit with their father’ (pitṛ-ṣad). A well-known example of such was ghoṣā.\nFoot notes Rv. ii. 17, 7; viii. 21, 15; x. 37, 3.\nRv. i. 117, 7. Cf. Zimmer, Altindisches Leben, 305.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["amaa", "amaa-jur", "amaajur", "amā", "amā-jur", "amājur", "jur", "अमा", "अमाजुर्", "जुर्", "అమా", "అమా-జుర్", "అమాజుర్", "జుర్", "ಅಮಾ", "ಅಮಾ-ಜುರ್", "ಅಮಾಜುರ್", "ಜುರ್"], "synonyms_slp1": ["amaa", "amaa-jur", "amaajur", "amā", "amā-jur", "amājur", "jur", "amA", "amAjur", "jur", "అమా", "అమా-జుర్", "అమాజుర్", "జుర్", "ಅಮಾ", "ಅಮಾ-ಜುರ್", "ಅಮಾ